.TITLE "mv"

.DIV "DESCRIPTION"
Moves or renames files and directories on macOS. This command relocates one or
more files or directories from a source to a destination. It can also be used
to rename a file or directory within the same location. It is a fundamental
utility for __filesystem management__.

.DIV "ALIAS"
**move**

.DIV "TLDR"
**mv __source_file__ __destination_file__**
Moves a __source_file__ to a __destination_file__. If the destination exists, it renames the file.
> __source_file__: The path to the file to move. | __destination_file__: The new path or name for the file.

**mv __old_name__ __new_name__**
Renames a file or directory from __old_name__ to __new_name__ within the same location.
> __old_name__: The current name of the file or directory. | __new_name__: The new name for the file or directory.

**mv __file1__ __file2__ __directory__**
Moves multiple files (__file1__, __file2__) into a specified __directory__.
> __file1__: First file to move. | __file2__: Second file to move. | __directory__: The destination directory.

**mv -v __source__ __destination__**
Moves a __source__ to a __destination__, showing verbose output.
> __-v__: verbose flag | __source__: The path to the file or directory to move. | __destination__: The new path or name for the file or directory.

.DIV "SUMMARY"
`mv` is a powerful command that can be destructive if used carelessly, as it
overwrites existing files at the destination without warning by default.
Use `mv -i` for interactive prompts before overwriting.

.DIV "SEE ALSO"
**mac:cp** **mac:rm**

